  "body": "## What\n\nReplaces the consecutive-count fallback eject (3 failures / 30s) with a rate-based policy that requires a sustained failure rate over a window before benching, and adds a per-route retry-once toggle for the primary. Both behaviors are configurable; a legacy mode preserves the old consecutive-count shape.\n\n## Why\n\nThe old consecutive-count rule was prone to false positives: a model that 2-3 transient 500s in a row got benched for 30s even when it was otherwise healthy, and the chain moved on before the real upstream error reached the client. The rate-based shape (50% over 10 requests, 8-sample floor, 3s bench) absorbs one-off blips on low-traffic models while still tripping fast on sustained failures, matching the mature-router pattern (LiteLLM / Portkey / OpenRouter). The retry-once toggle gives a one-shot chance to recover from genuinely transient errors (timeout, 5xx, 429, upstream) before falling through, which is what the user already had in spirit.\n\n## How\n\n- `core/credential_rotation.py`'s `RouteHealthRegistry` now supports `mode=\"consecutive\" | \"rate_based\"`. Rate-based uses a rolling window of outcomes (True=success, False=failure); a model benches when failures/window >= rate AND window >= min_samples. The `route_health_registry` factory's cache key expands from a 2-tuple to a 6-tuple of all six knobs, so changing any one starts a fresh registry.\n- `RouteExecutor.__init__` gains `retry_first` and `provider_lookup` params. The chain loop retries the primary once on a transient error when `retry_first=\"retry_once\"`, gated on position 0 and `_error_is_retryable()` (transient kinds only). The provider rate-limit skip reads `ProviderRateLimiter.remaining_wait()` via the executor's `provider_lookup` lambda, so a rate-limited provider is skipped at plan-build time without paying the wait.\n- `settings.py` adds 5 new `Field` entries (`fallback_behavior`, `fallback_eject_window`, `fallback_eject_failure_rate`, `fallback_eject_min_samples`, `fallback_retry_first`) with the documented defaults. `FALLBACK_EJECT_AFTER_FAILURES` is no longer read by the new eject logic; it remains valid as a deprecated alias for `FALLBACK_EJECT_AFTER_FAILURES` (the consecutive-mode knob) and emits a one-time warning if set.\n- `manifest.py` adds 5 new `ConfigFieldSpec` entries (2 selects, 3 numbers) so the new env vars are editable in the admin UI with the right types and descriptions. `FALLBACK_BEHAVIOR` and `FALLBACK_RETRY_FIRST` are selects with `legacy` / `rate_based` and `skip` / `retry_once` options respectively.\n- `.env.example` documents the 5 new vars with comments and updates the existing eject block to note that the consecutive settings are legacy-mode only.\n- `messages.py` and `responses.py` handlers thread the new params through to the executor, with a `_throttle_lookup` helper that resolves a provider and returns its `throttle_remaining()` (or None if unknown).\n- `docs/USAGE.md` updates the fallback settings table with the two new rows (Eject mode, Retry primary once) and one-paragraph explanations of each.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `ruff format` + `ruff check` — clean.\n- `ty check` — all checks passed.\n- `pytest tests/application/ tests/providers/ tests/contracts/` — **1923 passed** (includes import-boundary verification that `route_health.py` can import `parse_provider_type` from `config.model_refs`).\n- New tests: `test_rate_based_does_not_trip_below_min_samples`, `test_rate_based_trips_when_failure_rate_crosses_threshold`, `test_rate_based_does_not_trip_under_threshold`, `test_rate_based_provider_lookup_skips_throttled_models`, `test_consecutive_mode_is_preserved`, `test_error_is_retryable_classifies_failures`.\n- The two pre-existing bench tests in `test_execution.py` are explicitly pinned to `mode=\"consecutive\"` so their contract (3 consecutive benches) keeps working under the new default `rate_based` mode.\n\n## Residual risks\n\nNone known in the shipped code. The retry-once loop behavior is exercised by the `_error_is_retryable` unit test (classification) plus an end-to-end test planned separately; the chain-loop retry path itself is not mocked at the unit level because it requires a streaming provider mock. The provider rate-limit skip is wired but not asserted against a live 429 in this PR; will be covered by a stub-upstream e2e in a follow-up if you want it.\n\n## Defaults the user asked for\n\n- `FALLBACK_BEHAVIOR=rate_based` (was: implicit consecutive)\n- `FALLBACK_EJECT_WINDOW=10`\n- `FALLBACK_EJECT_FAILURE_RATE=0.5`\n- `FALLBACK_EJECT_MIN_SAMPLES=8`\n- `FALLBACK_EJECT_SECONDS=3` (was: 30)\n- `FALLBACK_RETRY_FIRST=skip` (was: n/a, now configurable)\n\nTo keep the old behavior exactly, set `FALLBACK_BEHAVIOR=legacy` in `.env`.\n\nVersion: 5.57.0 -> 5.58.0 (MINOR).",
