    "message": "feat(fallback): rate-based eject + retry-once toggle, with a legacy mode\n\nThe fallback chain's ejection was consecutive-count (3 failures / 30s),\nwhich benched a model after a single transient streak and left it there\nfor 30s regardless of whether the failure was real. That produced false\npositives on flapping providers and, because the chain moved on before\nsurfacing the failure, hid the real upstream error from the client.\n\nReplace it with a rate-based policy: a model is benched when at least\nFALLBACK_EJECT_FAILURE_RATE (default 50%) of its last FALLBACK_EJECT_WINDOW\n(default 10) requests failed, with at least FALLBACK_EJECT_MIN_SAMPLES\n(default 8) observed so a single blip never trips. The bench lasts\nFALLBACK_EJECT_SECONDS (default 3). A legacy mode preserves the old\nconsecutive-count behavior via FALLBACK_BEHAVIOR=legacy.\n\nAdd a per-route retry-once toggle (FALLBACK_RETRY_FIRST): when set to\nretry_once, a transient primary failure (timeout/5xx/429/upstream) gets one\nmore chance before the chain advances. Auth and invalid-request errors are\nnever retried. The retry applies only to position 0 (the primary).\n\nThe real upstream error now surfaces in the request_attempts log for every\nfailed attempt, and the chain steps over rate-limited providers at plan-\nbuild time via ProviderRateLimiter so the client never pays the wait.\n\nVerification: ruff format/check clean; ty check clean; full test suite\n1923 passed across tests/application, tests/providers, tests/contracts\n(including import boundaries). New tests cover rate-based math (trip,\nunder-threshold, window slide, below-min-samples, provider-skip),\nconsecutive-mode preservation, and _error_is_retryable classification.\n\nNot done: the retry-once loop behavior is not unit-tested end-to-end\n(requires a mock upstream stream); covered by the _error_is_retryable unit\ntest plus the e2e workflow. Provider-side rate-limit skip is wired but\nnot asserted against a live 429 here.\n\nBump 5.57.0 -> 5.58.0 (MINOR: new configurable behavior).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
