    "message": "fix(fallback): restore handler init and type-cast the eject key\n\nFollow-up to the rate-based-eject + retry-once PR (#195). Two issues\nsurfaced from the CI run on the first commit:\n\n1. The `_throttle_lookup` method was inserted at 8-space indent *inside*\n   `MessagesHandler.__init__`, which pulled the subsequent\n   `self._message_intercepts: tuple[MessageIntercept, ...] = (...)`\n   assignment into the nested def's body. The result was\n   `'MessagesHandler' object has no attribute '_message_intercepts'`\n   crashing every request, which cascaded into ~20 test_api_handlers\n   failures and the \"500 instead of 200\" results in test_api.py.\n\n   Move `_throttle_lookup` to class scope (4-space indent) and restore\n   the `_message_intercepts` assignment to its original place at the end\n   of `__init__`. The handler now initializes cleanly and\n   test_api_handlers passes 14/14. The same latent issue was already\n   fixed in `responses.py` by the earlier patch_fix6, so no change\n   there.\n\n2. The `route_health_registry` factory uses a 6-tuple cache key whose\n   first slot is the eject mode. The literal `key[0]` is inferred as\n   `str` (settings field) but the `RouteHealthRegistry.mode` parameter\n   expects `Literal[\"consecutive\",\"rate_based\"]`. Add a `_RouteEjectKey`\n   type alias, `cast(...)` the key at the dict access points, and\n   `cast(...)` the mode at the constructor call. The factory is\n   functionally unchanged; this is purely a type-narrowing assist that\n   satisfies `ty` (which is strict about `str` vs `Literal`).\n\nAlso: the pre-existing `test_api.py` failure baseline (14 failures:\n`test_model_mapping`, `KeyError: 'x-should-retry'`, provider errors\nreturning 500 instead of their upstream status) was verified to be\nidentical with and without these changes via a stash-and-rerun, so\nthose failures are NOT introduced by this PR -- they're a pre-existing\nlocal shell-environment baseline. CI is the real gate.\n\nVerification: ruff format --check clean; ruff check clean; ty check\nclean; test_api_handlers.py 14/14 passed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
