    "message": "fix(tests+fallback): harden provider_lookup against non-numeric return\nand wire the test_api.py fixture to actually inject the mock\n\nTwo changes that must land together, because the test fixture only\nworks once the production path it exercises is robust.\n\n1) src/my_claude_code/application/route_health.py -- the rate-based\neject path's provider_lookup branch did `if cooldown is not None and\ncooldown > 0` on the result of `provider.throttle_remaining()`. A\nprovider whose throttle_remaining() returns a non-numeric, non-None\nvalue (a MagicMock, a sentinel object, a future subclass that hasn't\nbeen updated) would raise a TypeError at plan-build time and abort the\nwhole chain. Guard with isinstance(cooldown, (int, float)) so anything\nunexpected is treated as \"unknown\" rather than blowing up. The default\nprovider in the test fixture returned exactly that (a MagicMock) and\nsurfaced the regression in CI.\n\n2) tests/api/test_api.py -- the fixture was patching\n`my_claude_code.api.routes.resolve_provider` and calling\nTestClient(app) where `app = create_test_app()` at module scope. The\npatch targeted a symbol the production path doesn't call (the chain\ngets its resolver from the RequestRuntimeLease, not from that route-\nlevel patch point), so the chain was making a real outbound HTTP call\nto the routed-to provider, which the test environment couldn't serve.\nRebuild the app per-fixture via `create_test_app(..., providers={...})`\nso the runtime factory injects the mock, restore the route-level patch\nas a belt-and-braces for the handler's resolver path, and add a module-\nlevel `app` so the one test that mutates `app.state` directly has\nsomething to mutate.\n\nVerification: ruff format --check + ruff check clean; ty check clean;\ntests/api/test_api.py 22/22 pass; the original rate-based-eject +\nretry-once tests (test_route_health + test_execution +\ntest_admin_limits) 153/153 still pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
